WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:03.459
When it comes to balancing performance

2
00:00:03.459 --> 00:00:06.099
versus visual fidelity, Unreal Engine gives you

3
00:00:06.099 --> 00:00:08.179
several avenues of control.

4
00:00:08.179 --> 00:00:10.580
For example, the quality of your volumetric fog

5
00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:13.099
can be increased substantially through changing

6
00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:16.719
various parameters and settings, as well as CVARs.

7
00:00:16.719 --> 00:00:19.420
Note, in certain situations, for example,

8
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:22.500
such as ICV effects versus video games,

9
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.539
opting for higher quality visuals

10
00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:27.579
over real-time performance may make sense.

11
00:00:27.579 --> 00:00:29.859
You can always profile your scene's performance

12
00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:32.539
by using stats, which are available from the viewport

13
00:00:32.539 --> 00:00:35.140
dropdown, or using CVARs.

14
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.380
A pro tip for getting the maximum visual quality output

15
00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.659
from your volumetric fog while still maintaining your editor

16
00:00:41.659 --> 00:00:45.420
performance is to add CVARs to your movie render

17
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.179
cue output settings.

18
00:00:47.179 --> 00:00:50.299
This will allow for the editor to use the cheaper, faster

19
00:00:50.299 --> 00:00:52.899
volumetric fog settings while using

20
00:00:52.899 --> 00:00:55.899
higher quality, slower performing settings when

21
00:00:55.899 --> 00:00:59.179
exporting from movie render cue.

22
00:00:59.179 --> 00:01:01.740
In the next video, we'll discuss using fog cards,

23
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.260
as well as localized fog volumes,

24
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.739
to create isolated areas of volumetrics.
